{
 "slug": "sum",
 "title": "The verb sum",
 "deck": "<em>sum, es, est</em>: \"I am\", \"you are\", \"he is\", and why the noun that goes with it is nominative.",
 "desc": "GCSE Latin grammar, Chapter 1: the irregular verb sum in the present tense, the complement in the nominative, and est and sunt for 'there is' and 'there are'.",
 "key": [
  [
   "<em>sum, es, est</em>",
   "I am, you are, he is"
  ],
  [
   "<em>sumus, estis, sunt</em>",
   "we are, you are, they are"
  ],
  [
   "After <em>sum</em>",
   "the nominative, never the accusative"
  ],
  [
   "<em>est, sunt</em> first",
   "often \"there is\", \"there are\""
  ]
 ],
 "marks": {
  "c": "the subject",
  "v": "the complement: nominative too"
 },
 "sections": [
  {
   "id": "what",
   "h2": "The verb \"to be\"",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "lead": "<em>sum</em> means \"I am\". It is irregular: it does not follow the pattern of <em>porto</em>, so it has to be learned on its own. It is also the commonest verb in Latin."
    },
    {
     "table": {
      "head": [
       "Person",
       "Latin",
       "English"
      ],
      "rows": [
       [
        "I",
        "<b>sum</b>",
        "I am"
       ],
       [
        "you (one)",
        "<b>es</b>",
        "you are"
       ],
       [
        "he, she, it",
        "<b>est</b>",
        "he is"
       ],
       [
        "we",
        "<b>sumus</b>",
        "we are"
       ],
       [
        "you (more than one)",
        "<b>estis</b>",
        "you are"
       ],
       [
        "they",
        "<b>sunt</b>",
        "they are"
       ]
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "note": "The endings are mostly the ones you know: <em>es</em>, <em>es-t</em>, <em>su-mus</em>, <em>es-tis</em>, <em>su-nt</em>. Only <em>sum</em> itself ends in <em>-m</em> instead of <em>-o</em>."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "complement",
   "h2": "The noun after sum",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "p": "<em>sum</em> does nothing to anyone, so it has no object. The noun that goes with it names the subject again, so it takes the same case as the subject, the <b>nominative</b>. This noun is called the <b>complement</b>."
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "<u class=\"c\">femina</u> <u class=\"v\">dea</u> est.",
      "en": "<b>The woman is a goddess.</b>",
      "note": "<em>dea</em> is nominative, like <em>femina</em>: they are the same person. Not <em>deam</em>."
     }
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "<u class=\"v\">servi</u> sumus.",
      "en": "<b>We are slaves.</b>",
      "note": "No separate word for \"we\": <em>sumus</em> has it. <em>servi</em> is nominative plural to match."
     }
    },
    {
     "facts": [
      "After <em>sum</em> the noun is <b>nominative</b>, never accusative.",
      "In English, \"a\" usually sounds best before the complement: \"he is a messenger\".",
      "<em>est</em> or <em>sunt</em> at the start of a sentence often means \"there is\" or \"there are\": <em>est nuntius</em>, \"there is a messenger\"."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "translate",
   "h2": "Translating sentences with sum",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "facts": [
      "Find the part of <em>sum</em> and its person, usually at the end.",
      "Translate the subject, then \"is\" or \"are\", then the complement.",
      "If there is only one nominative, the subject is inside the verb: <em>nuntius est</em>, \"he is a messenger\"."
     ]
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "<u class=\"c\">amici</u> <u class=\"v\">nuntii</u> non sunt.",
      "en": "<b>The friends are not messengers.</b>",
      "note": "<em>non</em> sits before the verb, as with any other verb."
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "watch",
   "h2": "Watch out",
   "exam": true,
   "label": "Common mistakes",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "facts": [
      "The complement is nominative: \"the woman is a goddess\" is <em>femina dea est</em>, not <em>deam</em>.",
      "<em>est</em> is \"is\" and <em>sunt</em> is \"are\": check whether the subject is singular or plural.",
      "<em>es</em> is \"you are\", for one person. It is not \"he is\"."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
